VT_TALK("sql/sql-select", {
 "columns": "A SELECT list is a list of expressions, and each one is worked out again for every row. It can be a plain column, a fixed value or a small calculation. A shopkeeper who wants to know how much money is sitting on each shelf multiplies price by stock, and SQL does that in one line with price times stock. You can add, subtract, multiply, divide and take the remainder, and two vertical bars join pieces of text together. Watch out for division. In SQLite and PostgreSQL, seven divided by two gives three, because both numbers are whole. Write two point zero to get three and a half. Think of each row passing a calculator that applies the same formula.",
 "alias": "An alias is a nickname. Writing AS after a column gives the result column a friendlier name, and later we will give whole tables short nicknames too. Text values always go in single quotes, such as the word Pune. Double quotes mean a name, like a column or table name, and they let you use a name that clashes with a keyword. If your text contains an apostrophe, write two single quotes in a row. Two dashes start a comment that runs to the end of the line. SELECT even works with no table at all, which makes it a handy calculator. Think of an alias as the name card on a lunch box, it does not change the food, only how you find it.",
 "distinct": "Sometimes the same value appears many times, like Pune for several customers. DISTINCT removes repeated rows so each one appears once, and it judges the whole row of chosen columns. LIMIT keeps only the first few rows, and OFFSET skips some rows before that. This is exactly how page two of a shopping site works, with limit twenty and offset twenty. One rule matters a lot here. Without ORDER BY, the database gives rows in any order it likes, so the first three means nothing. Sort first, then limit. Other engines spell it differently, such as FETCH FIRST in the standard and TOP in SQL Server. Think of a queue, you must line people up before you can say who is first.",
 "levels": "Here is the same idea at four depths. For a child, SELECT is a photocopier that copies only the columns you point at, with a tiny calculator for each line. At college level, the select list is worked out for each row, then DISTINCT, ORDER BY and LIMIT shape the final list. At masters level, the select list is projection in relational algebra, and SQL keeps duplicates unless you ask for DISTINCT. At research and production level, big offsets are slow because skipped rows are still built and thrown away, so real systems remember the last key they showed. Think of reading a long book by remembering your page number instead of counting pages from the start every time."
});
